          pure functions

    Negative-direction witness — joining per-variable
    EffectClass values into EffectSummary rejected:

      Take Row E13 (per_variable_effectclass_does_not_poison).
      Suppose hypothetical implementer derives EffectSummary
      by joining per-variable EffectClass values from the
      AimsStateMap (treating per-variable effects as if they
      were per-function):
        for var in func.variables {
            let var_effect = state_map.var_states[var].effect;
            if var_effect == EffectClass::ALL {
                effects.may_allocate = true;
                effects.may_deallocate = true;
                effects.may_share = true;
                effects.may_throw = true;
                effects.may_read_inaccessible = true;
            }
        }

      Then any function with an Apply-no-contract call (which
      sets the call result's per-variable EffectClass to ALL
      per TF-5) would have its EffectSummary widened to ALL,
      regardless of whether the call result is actually used
      (e.g., Cardinality = Absent — the result is dead).

      This does not license a memory optimization: RL-30 independently marks
      every untyped call may_write_inaccessible and therefore ReadWrite,
      regardless of result liveness. The separation exists so ownership/FIP
      effects are not polluted by a per-variable result lattice.

      Per §AIMS §3.7 CRITICAL note: "Per-variable EffectClass =
      ALL on call results does NOT poison caller's
      EffectSummary." The architectural separation is
      load-bearing for the AIMS mission ("RC rare, not RC
      fast" per Annex E §AIMS §1) — IC-5's per-instruction
      derivation discipline IS what enables the lattice to
      prove pure functions PURE despite Apply-no-contract
      calls in the middle of them. (P1) preserved by the
      shipped engine's pattern-match-on-instruction-shape
      discipline; corrupting it to join-per-variable would destroy the
      lattice's ability to certify ownership/FIP properties.

    Negative-direction witness — replacing OR with AND on
    may_share rejected:

      Take Row E5 (apply_with_known_contract_inherits).
      Suppose hypothetical implementer makes the may_share
      join AND instead of OR:
        may_share: self.may_share && other.may_share

      Then inheriting from a sharing callee would CLEAR the
      caller's `may_share` (when the caller's pre-inherit
      value is false). Worse: the AND-identity is `true`, so
      starting at `OPTIMISTIC.may_share = false` would mean
      EVERY call to a sharing callee clears `may_share` to
      false (since `false && X = false` for any X).

      Per L-6 monotonicity: the corrupted AND-join is
      anti-monotone in the OR-lattice direction. Functions
      sharing through callees would silently report
      `may_share = false`. The corrupted contract would then let ownership
      realization omit required sharing and RC operations. RL-30 still fails
      closed on the call and does not emit an argument-region-only attribute.

      Result: silent miscompilation — exactly the AIMS
      Invariant 1 ("Contracts and realization must agree" per
      Annex E §AIMS §2 invariant 1) violation.
      OR-join for the effect fields is therefore load-bearing for AIMS contract
      and realization agreement; AND-join is a soundness failure.

    Negative-direction witness — replacing AND with OR on
    alloc_only_on_slow_path rejected:

      Take Row E16
      (post_realization_unconditional_alloc_clears_slow_path).
      Suppose hypothetical implementer makes the
      `alloc_only_on_slow_path` join OR instead of AND:
        alloc_only_on_slow_path: self.alloc_only_on_slow_path
                              || other.alloc_only_on_slow_path

      Then a function with one unconditional alloc + one
      slow-path alloc would inherit `alloc_only_on_slow_path
      = true` from the slow-path-only callee, overriding the
      caller's own unconditional alloc that should clear the
      claim.

      Per the spec semantics (sec-5 IC-5): the claim "alloc
      only on slow path" is universal — every alloc in the
      function MUST be on a slow path for the claim to hold.
      AND IS the universal-claim operator; OR is the
      existential-claim operator. Substituting OR yields an
      EXISTENTIAL claim: "some alloc is on a slow path",
      which is much weaker than the spec rule's UNIVERSAL
      claim.

      Result: FIP-Conditional certification (per Annex E §AIMS IC-6 "When `may_allocate && alloc_only_on_slow_path`,
      the function is FIP-eligible with Conditional
      preconditions") would fire on functions with
      unconditional allocs that don't satisfy the
      precondition. Downstream Step 5a `verify_fip_contract`
      would then reject the cert, but only AFTER the contract
      has been used by upstream callers — wasted convergence
      iterations + diagnostic noise.

      AND-join for `alloc_only_on_slow_path` is therefore
      load-bearing for the FIP-Conditional discipline; OR-join
      is a semantic-mismatch failure (wrong quantifier).

    IC-5 dependencies — central within AIMS contract layer:

      IC-5 consumes the function's instruction stream + the
      converged ContractMap (filled in by prior SCC iterations
      + IC-3 + IC-4) + the AimsStateMap's exclusion predicate
      (for immortal / scalar / value-marker filtering). It
      produces a per-function EffectSummary. The dependency
      chain at the proof-obligation level is acyclic:
        IC-1 --> IC-2 (parameter contract seeding)
        IC-2 --> IC-3 (parameter contract componentwise join)
        IC-3 --> IC-4 (return contract componentwise join)
        IC-3 --> IC-5 (effect summary OR-join via callee
                         inheritance; uses ContractMap state
                         joined per IC-3)
        IC-4 --> IC-5 (effect summary may share callee
                         inheritance path with IC-4's
                         ReturnContract refinement —
                         independent dimensions but co-
                         iterated)
        L-1/L-2/L-6 (sec-02 lattice properties) --> IC-5
        IC-5 --> IC-6 (FipContract derivation reads
                         EffectSummary to classify
                         Never/Conditional/Bounded/Certified)
        IC-5 --> IC-7 (convergence bound — consumes
                         EffectSummary chain height = 6 to
                         compute iteration bound)
        IC-5 --> RL-30 (neutral MemoryAccessFact consumes EffectSummary plus
                         parameter contracts and realized-operation write facts)

      IC-5 is the effect-derivation operator within the IC
      layer; L-* lemmas are proven upstream in sec-02.

    Algorithm-citation declaration:
      algorithm: per-instruction OR-fold + AND-fold +
                           one-shot SCC-derived assignment;
                           callee EffectSummary inheritance via
                           componentwise OR-join on 5 fields
                           and componentwise AND-join on
                           alloc_only_on_slow_path; architectural
                           separation from per-variable
                           EffectClass lattice dimension
      published basis: Lorenzen, A., Leijen, D., Reinking, A.,
                           "FP²: Fully In-Place Functional
                           Programming Provides Memory Reuse for
                           Pure Functional Programs", ICFP 2023
                           (Theorem 2: `may_allocate == false ∧
                           may_deallocate == false ⟹ FIP; 6
                           independent effect flags ground in
                           the FP² paper); Cousot & Cousot,
                           "Abstract Interpretation: A Unified
                           Lattice Model for Static Analysis of
                           Programs by Construction or
                           Approximation of Fixpoints", POPL
                           1977 (per-function effect summary
                           computation via SCC-topological
                           monotone iteration); Reynolds, J. C.,
                           "Definitional Interpreters for
                           Higher-Order Programming Languages",
                           Higher-Order and Symbolic Computation,
                           vol. 11, no. 4, 1998 (effect-system
                           theoretical foundation —
                           per-function effect signature
                           independent of per-program-point
                           state)
      complexity: O(N + M + C) per function, where N
                           is the instruction count (across all
                           blocks), M is the terminator count,
                           and C is the call-site count
                           (ContractMap lookups via `sigs.get`);
                           O(param_count × call_site_count ×
                           height_sum) total per SCC convergence
                           per IC-7's bound
      determinism: deterministic; per-instruction
                           pattern-match is a pure function of
                           the ArcFunction IR + ContractMap;
                           block + instruction visitation order
                           is consistent across compilation runs
                           (Salsa-stable); commutativity (L-1)
                           + associativity (L-2) ensure
                           fold-order independence per sec-6
                           IA-9
      reference impl: `accumulate_instr_effects` at
                           compiler/ori_arc/src/
                           aims/intraprocedural/effects.rs lines
                           22-62;
                           `accumulate_terminator_effects` lines
                           68-79; finalization in
                           `extract_contract` at
                           compiler/ori_arc/src/
                           aims/interprocedural/extract.rs line
                           157 (has_unbounded_stack one-shot);
                           join operator at
                           contract/mod.rs lines 535-548;
                           oracle re-derivation at
                           aims/verify/oracle.rs line 288
                           (`derive_effects`, used by VF-3
                           contract cross-check per
                           Annex E §AIMS)
      cross-language Koka Perceus
      prior art: (`koka/src/Core/CheckFBIP.hs`) derives
                           per-function FBIP effect annotations
                           via similar per-instruction pattern-
                           match; Swift SILOptimizer's
                           `EffectsAttr` carries
                           argmemonly / readonly / readnone
                           attributes derived from
                           SIL-instruction-shape analysis;
                           Lean 4 Borrow.lean computes per-
                           function effect summaries via
                           similar SCC-based per-instruction
                           accumulation; FP² paper's Theorem 2
                           formalizes the FBIP/FIP effect-
                           system shape that AIMS implements;
                           the published reference-compiler corpus cites Lean
                           4 + Swift ARC + Koka Perceus as the
                           prior art set

